===Mastery Strategy===
{{MastComp|{{MastStrat|first|Active|-60}}|{{MastStrat|second|Collect and use}}|{{MastStrat|third|Play with Toons}}|{{MastStrat|fourth|Machine}}|{{MastStrat|fifth|Reach/do floors}}|{{MastStrat|sixth|Meters|3Star}}}}
==Requirement Strategies==
*For the first requirement, focus on picking up [[Research Capsules]] and extracting [[Machines]]. The Machine and Capsule count increases the further you descend, so you can earn more {{II|Ichor|name=true}}. To maximize Ichor, It's heavily recommended to play solo, allowing you to gain larger amounts from extracting, whereas in a team where other extractors may be present. Playing as a [[Toon]] with an Active [[Ability]] that can be used without any perquisites will give you 5 Ichor every Floor.
*For the second requirement, have a distractor Toon bring {{CI|Twisted Shelly|name=true}} to the elevator to you so you can get aggro. Playing as {{CI|Rodger|name=true}} also helps getting Research faster because of his Passive [[Ability]]. This will be easier if Twisted Shelly is on the [[Daily Twisted Board]].
*For the third requirement, play as {{CI|Tisha|name=true}} and try to complete her [[Mastery]] Quests by going on multiple runs. It is recommended to go on an organized Run or do specific quests in certain runs.

==Strategies==
Shelly has the highest [[Skill Check]] [[Stat]] available, being 5{{Star}}, which gives her an aptitude for extraction. Her Active [[Ability]], {{AI|Shelly|name=true}}, is capable of drastically increasing the [[Extraction Speed]] of a selected [[Toon]] by a whole 75%. As Shelly, you can either find an extractor using or near a [[Machine]] and boost them, or go off on your own and extract Machines, boosting those that pass you on the off-chance that they'll stumble across a Machine before the boost wears off. Upon any Machine's completion, her Passive, '''Problem Solver''', solves one of her other problems, that being slightly-below average movement stats that hinder travel by giving her a boost of 25% in [[Movement Speed]] until the 10 seconds wear off (18.75 Walk Speed/31.25 Run Speed). This boost can stack.

Shelly's '''Inspiration''' boost is really effective, being able to make 1-2 Star Extraction Speed Toons extract almost as fast as a 5 Star Extraction Speed Toon. Generally, unless duoing, it's best to boost anyone extracting that you can find on low Floors, though note that with multiple targets, you have to think carefully about who you are boosting, like prioritizing a Toon that has just started a Machine over one that's nearly completing it. Toons with high extraction Stats get to have more out of the buff as it lets them find another Machine to complete faster, but if most of the Machines are done, the Floor will finish faster if you buff a slow extractor or the person with the least completion on their Machine. If you're being chased, you can buff a teammate who's almost done with a Machine to get a speed boost out of it. Shelly's boost also stacks with other Extraction Speed buffs, so it can turn things like {{II|Instructions|name=false}} or {{TI|Stress Ball|name=true}} into a real force if you've seen someone bring them or if a Machine is in a risky spot. Lastly, don't buff a Toon that's priming, as it could mess them up. To make the best use out of her Active, memorizing Machine placements is very helpful. Doing this can help you predict if a Toon notices and wants to extract a Machine, therefore giving them a boost earlier.

Shelly's Passive Ability, '''Problem Solver''', is a great survival tool for Shelly given the amazing speeds it gives when multiple Machines are finished at once, with 5 Machines completed allowing her to outwalk {{CI|Twisted Dyle|name=true}} outside of [[Panic Mode]]. To see these speeds in full for every Machine that can be possibly finished at a time, see the table below.
{{SFSpeedTable|Shelly}}

In organized runs such as 'Mach Crushers' (runs with 4+ Shellies), a strategy called Duoing is used to capitalize on Shelly's strengths and cooldown, though you can do this if you have a partner to play with you. Duoing has pairs of Shellies stay together, prioritizing one side of the map (left or right) to cover the most ground without being redundant. They try to only boost each other unless there is a Machine near the distractor in need of completing, they can't find their duo/an extraction Machine, or other teammates are doing the last Machines. This strategy is used in more experienced runs and becomes very productive if {{TI|Vee's Remote|name=true}} is used, as the Shellies can combo with each other early on. Generally, one Shelly "leads", using Vee's Remote first, and the other finds their own Machine to do. Then, when the leader finds another Machine, they boost each other, with the other finding their own Machine. This helps to avoid confusion and to utilize your cooldowns well. Also, Shelly's Passive reduces the need to prime, speeding up Floors further. Again, note that this strategy is used mostly in more organized runs. Duos can also match [[Skins]] to coordinate better, but matching colors reduces ambiguity (i.e. green for {{Skin|Fall Stroll}} and {{Skin|Prehistoric Peppermint}}).

Shelly can pair up really well with {{CI|Brusha|name=true}} for many reasons. For one, a painted Machine's 25% boost stacks with Shelly's 75% boost, totaling to a whopping 118.8%, meaning a team would practically have an infinite supply of Instructions and more. Plus both of their boosts really work with each other's Stat spreads.

Shelly's main weakness is her low [[Stamina]] stat. She just has 2 Star Stamina (125 units), resulting in some troubles with Stamina management if not careful. It's recommended to not get below 50 units unless you have some sort of support (an Item, support Toons, experience, or reliable distractors). However, the Stamina problem becomes mitigated once she gains the 70 extra units of Stamina from [[Dyle's Floor]] and both Stamina [[Cards]], putting her at 195 units. Even though her Movement Speed is average while ignoring her Passive, she will have trouble running from [[Twisteds]] that outpace her in prolonged chases.

As for Items, because she leans towards Extraction, a very obvious choice would be picking and using Extraction Items, allowing her to extract Machines faster for the team. Stamina and Speed Items are good choices too, helping her extract [[Treadmill Machines]] with or without her Passive and keep her Stamina while searching for Machines or for urgent situations, like needing to get to the Elevator while extracting far from it. Generally, Shelly benefits from all Items, so don't be shy to pick some up if you don't have a {{CI|Sprout|name=true}}. Like every other Toon, she benefits from healing, but as a {{Type|Main}}, {{II|Bandage|name=false}}s are better over {{II|Health Kit|name=false}}s. Unless you can rely on others for support, it's a good idea to preserve at least one slot for escape Items.

